"Southern District of New York | Two Robinhood Employees Charged With Fraud | United States Department of Justice (https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdny/pr/two-robinhood-employees-charged-fraud)\nciteturn50view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdny/pr/two-robinhood-employees-charged-fraud\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 121\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Image: open menu icon】 【2†Image: open menu icon】 DOJ Menu\nL3: \nL4: 【3†U.S. Department of Justice】\nL5:   * About\nL6:     * 【4†The Attorney General】\nL7:     * 【5†Organizational Chart】\nL8:     * 【6†Budget & Performance】\nL9:     * 【7†History & Museum】\nL10:     * 【8†Privacy Program】\nL11:   * 【9†Our Work】\nL12:   * News\nL13:     * 【10†Press Releases】\nL14:     * 【11†Speeches】\nL15:     * 【12†Videos】\nL16:     * 【13†Photo Galleries】\nL17:     * 【14†Blogs】\nL18:     * 【15†Podcasts】\nL19:   * Resources\nL20:     * 【16†Guidance Documents】\nL21:     * 【17†Forms】\nL22:     * 【18†Publications】\nL23:     * 【19†Information for Victims in Large Cases】\nL24:     * 【20†Justice Manual】\nL25:     * 【21†Business and Contracts】\nL26:   * 【22†Grants】\nL27:   * Employment\nL28:     * 【23†Why Justice ?】\nL29:     * 【24†Benefits】\nL30:     * 【25†DOJ Vacancies】\nL31:     * 【26†Legal Careers at DOJ】\nL32:   * 【27†About SDNY】\nL33:   * 【28†Find Help】\nL34:   * 【29†Contact Us】\nL35: \nL36: [Input] 【1†Image: open menu icon】 【2†Image: close menu icon】 Menu Close\nL37: \nL38: Search [Input] [Input: Search] 【30†Image: Search】\nL39: \nL40: Press Release\nL41: # Two Robinhood Employees Charged With Fraud\nL42: \nL43: Tuesday, September 15, 2026\nL44: \nL45: For Immediate Release\nL46: \nL47: U.S. Attorney's Office, Southern District of New York\nL48: \nL49: Robinhood Engineers Hefu Chai and Huaisong Xiang Traded Perpetual Futures on the Basis of Confidential Business Information\nL50: United States Attorney for the Southern District of New York, Jamie McDonald, and Assistant Director in Charge of the New York Field Office of the Federal Bureau of Investigation (“FBI”), James C. Barnacle, Jr., announced today the unsealing of Complaints charging HEFU CHAI and HUAISONG XIANG, a/k/a “Jerry Xiang,” with commodities fraud and wire fraud arising from a scheme to misappropriate confidential business information from their employer, Robinhood Markets, Inc.\nL51: (“Robinhood”), and use that information to trade perpetual futures on Hyperliquid, a decentralized derivatives exchange. CHAI will be presented today in the Northern District of California. XIANG will be presented today before U.S. Magistrate Judge Ona T. Wang.\nL52: > “Misappropriating confidential information to trade in the derivatives markets for personal benefit is illegal,” said U.S. Attorney Jamie McDonald. “That is exactly what we allege Hefu Chai and Huaisong Xiang have done. Today’s charges make clear that corporate insiders cannot evade the securities and commodities laws by trading based on misappropriated information in derivatives like perpetual futures, tokenized securities, or other similar financial instruments.”\nL53: “Hefu Chai and Huaisong Xiang are charged with commodities fraud and wire fraud for allegedly exploiting confidential business information taken from their employer to trade perpetual futures,” said FBI Assistant Director in Charge James C. Barnacle, Jr. “These charges make clear the FBI with its partners will act when individuals access sensitive business information for their own benefit.”\nL54: \nL55: According to the Complaints unsealed today in Manhattan federal court:(1)\nL56: CHAI and XIANG were employed as engineers at Robinhood. In connection with their roles at Robinhood, CHAI and XIANG had access to nonpublic information regarding whether and when Robinhood would support additional cryptocurrencies for trading on Robinhood Crypto, a Robinhood subsidiary that operates a digital asset trading platform.\nL57: Hyperliquid markets itself as a decentralized exchange for trading a wide range of derivative products, including cryptocurrencies and derivatives tied to other underlying assets. Investors on Hyperliquid can purchase, among other things, perpetual futures (“perpetuals”). Perpetuals are derivative products that allow investors to take positions on the price movements of an underlying asset, whether a cryptocurrency or another asset, without owning the asset itself.\nL58: Unlike traditional futures contracts, Hyperliquid’s perpetuals do not expire and can be maintained indefinitely. To keep a position open, however, traders must make or receive periodic funding payments, which align the perpetual’s price with the spot price of the underlying asset. A trader can settle the perpetual at any time and realize their gain or loss.\nL59: Between 2025 and 2026, CHAI and XIANG repeatedly bought perpetual futures linked to cryptocurrency tokens on Hyperliquid in advance of Robinhood’s public announcements that the underlying cryptocurrency tokens would be listed on Robinhood Crypto.\nL60: On each occasion, CHAI and XIANG possessed material nonpublic information that Robinhood would list the cryptocurrency token on Robinhood Crypto, and CHAI and XIANG traded on that information in breach of the duties that CHAI and XIANG owed to Robinhood to maintain the confidentiality of this information. CHAI and XIANG each profited more than $50,000 from their illicit trading.\nL61: *                *                *\nL62: \nL63: CHAI, 36, of Menlo Park, California, and XIANG, 30, of Jersey City, New Jersey, are charged with one count of violating the Commodity Exchange Act, which carries a maximum sentence of 10 years in prison, and one count of wire fraud, which carries a maximum sentence of 20 years in prison.\nL64: The maximum potential sentences in this case are prescribed by Congress and provided here for informational purposes only, as any sentencing of the defendants will be determined by the judge.\nL65: \nL66: Mr. McDonald praised the outstanding work of the FBI. Mr. McDonald further thanked Robinhood for its cooperation with the investigation.\nL67: \nL68: This case is being handled by the Office’s Securities and Commodities Fraud Task Force. Assistant U.S. Attorney Alexandra N. Rothman is in charge of the prosecution.\nL69: The charges contained in the Complaints are merely accusations, and the defendants are presumed innocent unless and until proven guilty.\nL70: \nL71:   1. 【31†^】\nL72: \nL73:  As the introductory phrase signifies, the entirety of the text of the Complaints and the description of the Complaints set forth herein constitute only allegations, and every fact described therein should be treated as an allegation.\nL74: \nL75: Contact\nL76: \nL77: Nicholas Biase, Shelby Wratchford\nL78: (212) 637-2600\nL79: \nL80: Updated September 15, 2026\nL81: \nL82: * * *\nL83: \nL84: Attachments\nL85: 【32†26_mag_3716_chai_complaint.pdf】\nL86: \nL87: 【33†26_mag_3718_xiang_complaint.pdf】\nL88: \nL89: Topic\nL90: \nL91: Securities, Commodities, & Investment Fraud\nL92: \nL93: Component\nL94: \nL95: 【34†USAO - New York, Southern】\nL96: \nL97: Press Release Number: 26-260\nL98: ## Related Content\nL99: \nL100: Press Release\nL101: \nL102: 【35†Kalder CEO Sentenced To 25 Months In Prison】\nL103: \nL104: United States Attorney for the Southern District of New York, Jamie McDonald, announced today that GÖKÇE GÜVEN, the founder and former Chief Executive Officer of Kalder Inc. (“Kalder”), was sentenced...\nL105: \nL106: October 30, 2026\nL107: \nL108: Press Release\nL109: \nL110: 【36†‘Pre-IPO’ Boiler Room Operators Sentenced To Prison】\nL111: United States Attorney for the Southern District of New York, Jamie McDonald, announced today that the founders and operators of Legend Venture Partners LLC (“Legend”) and its affiliated entities were...\nL112: \nL113: September 30, 2026\nL114: \nL115: Press Release\nL116: \nL117: 【37†Two Men Charged In Scheme To Defraud Investors Seeking To Invest In Pre-Ipo Companies And Trading Fund】\nL118: United States Attorney for the Southern District of New York, Jamie McDonald, and the Acting Inspector in Charge of the New York Office of the U.S. Postal Inspection Service (“USPIS”), Darnell...\nL119: \nL120: September 29, 2026\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQ3 2026 Robinhood  Amended Exhibit 99.1 (https://robinhood.gcs-web.com/static-files/097e3ef5-f1a9-4981-b224-c58d9da67159)\nciteturn50view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 18; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://robinhood.gcs-web.com/static-files/097e3ef5-f1a9-4981-b224-c58d9da67159\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 693\nL0@P0: Robinhood Reports Third Quarter 2026 Results\nL1@P0: Revenues were a record $1.4 billion, up 12% year-over-year\nL2@P0: Diluted EPS down 16% year-over-year to $0.51; Adjusted EPS down 3% year-over-year to $0.60\nL3@P0: Net Deposits were $16 billion, and Robinhood Gold Subscribers reached a record 5.2 million\nL4@P0: Robinhood now up to 14 business lines that have reached $100 million or more in annualized revenues\nL5@P0: MENLO PARK, Calif. – October 27, 2026 – Robinhood Markets, Inc. (“Robinhood”) (NASDAQ: HOOD)\nL6@P0: today announced financial results for the third quarter of 2026, which ended September 30, 2026.\nL7@P0: \"Our vision is to make everyone an owner, because broad ownership is essential to a free, stable, and prosperous\nL8@P0: society,\" said Vlad Tenev, Chairman and CEO of Robinhood.\nL9@P0: \"Customers are trusting us with more of their assets as we deliver on our product roadmap, leading to our highest\nL10@P0: quarter ever for revenues,\" said Shiv Verma, Chief Financial Officer of Robinhood. \"We also added a new\nL11@P0: business cylinder with over $100 million in annualized revenues this quarter as Rothera scaled rapidly. And we're\nL12@P0: entering Q4 with strong momentum, as October Net Deposits have already topped $13 billion month-to-date,\nL13@P0: including $6 billion from the Dell family's contribution to Trump Accounts.\"\nL14@P0: Third Quarter Results\nL15@P0: • Total net revenues increased 12% year-over-year to $1.42 billion.\nL16@P0: ◦ Transaction-based revenues increased 19% year-over-year to $867 million, primarily driven by event\nL17@P0: contracts revenue of $173 million, up over 6x, options revenue of $399 million, up 31%, and equities\nL18@P0: revenue of $121 million, up 41%, partially offset by cryptocurrencies revenue of $120 million, down 55%.\nL19@P0: ◦ Net interest revenues decreased 9% year-over-year to $415 million, primarily driven by lower securities\nL20@P0: lending activity and short-term interest rates, partially offset by growth in interest-earning assets.\nL21@P0: ◦ Other revenues increased 61% year-over-year to $142 million, primarily driven by Trump Account\nL22@P0: service revenues and increased Robinhood Gold subscription revenues.\nL23@P0: • Net income decreased 14% year-over-year to $476 million, and diluted earnings per share (\"EPS\")\nL24@P0: decreased 16% to $0.51.\nL25@P0: ◦ Net income included $67 million, or $0.07 of diluted EPS, of unrealized losses primarily related to the\nL26@P0: mark-to-market of Robinhood Ventures Fund I (\"RVI\").\nL27@P0: • Adjusted Net Income (non-GAAP) decreased 3% year-over-year to $542 million, and Adjusted EPS\nL28@P0: (non-GAAP) decreased 3% to $0.60.\nL29@P0: ◦ Adjusted Net Income and Adjusted EPS are new non-GAAP financial measures introduced this quarter\nL30@P0: to provide a view of profitability before certain non-operating items; refer to page 13 for more information.\nL31@P0: • Total operating expenses increased 16% year-over-year to $739 million. The year-over-year increase was\nL32@P0: primarily driven by higher provisions for credit losses as we grow the credit card loan book, expenses\nL33@P0: related to Trump Accounts and Rothera, and marketing and growth investments.\nL34@P0: ◦ Adjusted Operating Expenses and Share-Based Compensation (“SBC”) (non-GAAP) increased 8%\nL35@P0: year-over-year to $664 million.\nL36@P0: • Adjusted EBITDA (non-GAAP) increased 9% year-over-year to $808 million.\nL37@P0: • Funded Customers increased by 1.9 million, or 7%, year-over-year to 28.7 million.\nL38@P0: ◦ Investment Accounts increased by 2.5 million , or 9%, year-over-year to 30.4 million.\nL39@P0: • Total Platform Assets increased 19% year-over-year to $396  billion, primarily driven by continued Net\nL40@P0: Deposits and higher equity valuations, partially offset by lower cryptocurrency valuations.\nL41@P0: • Net Deposits were $15.7 billion, an annualized growth rate of 17% relative to Total Platform Assets at the\nL42@P0: end of Q2 2026. Over the past twelve months, Net Deposits were $71.0  billion, a growth rate of 21%\nL43@P0: relative to Total Platform Assets at the end of Q3 2025.\nL44@P0: • Robinhood Gold Subscribers increased by 1.3 million, or 34%, year-over-year to 5.2 million.\nL45@P0: • Average Revenue Per User (“ARPU”) increased 5% year-over-year to $200.\nL46@P0: • Cash and cash equivalents totaled $5.8 billion, compared with $4.3 billion at the end of Q3 2025.\nL47@P0: • Share repurchases were $25 million, representing 0.2 million shares of our Class A common stock at an\nL48@P0: average price per share of approximately $105.\nL49@P0: ◦ Through Q3 2026, cumulative repurchases under our program since Q3 2024 total $1.3 billion,\nL50@P0: representing 27 million of our Class A shares at an average price per share of approximately $48.\nL51@P0-1: 1Highlights\nL52@P1: Robinhood Delivers Growth Across the Business as It Expands Global Access to Financial Markets\nL53@P1: #1 Platform for Active Traders\nL54@P1: • Market share gains drove active trader engagement to new highs in Q3, with record volumes for equities,\nL55@P1: options, and prediction markets, and record margin balances.\nL56@P1: • In September, Robinhood hosted its third annual HOOD Summit in Houston, with about 1,500 in-person\nL57@P1: attendees and more than 50 million livestream viewers. At the event, the company announced 17 new\nL58@P1: products and features aimed at making it the best platform for active traders, including Robinhood Agents\nL59@P1: and Agent Apps, 24/7 trading of select U.S. equities, perpetual futures for eligible U.S. customers, and\nL60@P1: earnings contracts in the prediction markets hub.\nL61@P1: • To date, approximately 200 thousand customers have opened Agentic Trading Accounts, driving over $1.6\nL62@P1: billion in notional trading volume.\nL63@P1: • Rothera reached over $100 million in annualized revenues in its first full quarter of operation, with over 6.4\nL64@P1: billion contracts traded since launch.\nL65@P1: #1 in Wallet Share for the Next Generation\nL66@P1: • Trump Accounts reached another major milestone as the U.S. Treasury launched automatic enrollment,\nL67@P1: establishing accounts for nearly 70 million children nationwide. Additionally, 25 million children have $250\nL68@P1: invested thanks to Susan and Michael Dell's $6.25 billion pledge.\nL69@P1: • In Q3, Gold subscribers grew to a record 5.2 million, representing an 18 percent overall adoption rate and\nL70@P1: approximately 40 percent adoption rate among new customers.\nL71@P1: • Robinhood Gold Card grew to 1.3 million customers with over $19 billion in total annualized payment\nL72@P1: volume in Q3.\nL73@P1: • Robinhood Banking continued to drive momentum, with nearly $5 billion in deposits from over 350 thousand\nL74@P1: Funded Customers and approximately 40 percent of customers signed up for direct deposit to date.\nL75@P1: • Robinhood Strategies grew to 350 thousand Funded Customers with over $2 billion in assets under\nL76@P1: management in Q3.\nL77@P1: • Trust Accounts are over $300 million in assets with an average account size of over $400 thousand to date.\nL78@P1: #1 Global Financial Ecosystem\nL79@P1: • Robinhood continued to grow its global footprint, launching crypto trading in the UK and announcing it has\nL80@P1: received authorization from the Dubai Financial Services Authority (\"DFSA\") to launch in the region. The\nL81@P1: authorization covers trading and custody across all ten DFSA-approved asset classes, including equities,\nL82@P1: crypto, ETFs, and investment tokens. Robinhood plans to begin rolling out its product suite in the area in\nL83@P1: 2027.\nL84@P1: • In its first quarter on Mainnet, Robinhood Chain became a top-five chain by revenue and volume,\nL85@P1: generating over $80 billion in DEX volume and over $50 million of chain fees.\nL86@P1-2: 2Additional Q3 2026 Operating Data\nL87@P2: • Robinhood Retirement AUC increased 51% year-over-year to a record $36.6 billion.\nL88@P2: • Margin Book increased 59% year-over-year to a record $22.1 billion.\nL89@P2: • Cash and Deposits increased 43% year-over-year to a record $20.4 billion.\nL90@P2: • Cash Sweep decreased 9% year-over-year to $32.3 billion.\nL91@P2: • Equity Notional Trading Volumes increased 53% year-over-year to a record $992 billion.\nL92@P2: • Options Contracts Traded increased 48% year-over-year to a record 905 million.\nL93@P2: • Crypto Notional Trading Volumes decreased 38% year-over-year to $50 billion, including Robinhood App\nL94@P2: Notional Volumes which decreased 48% year-over-year to $21 billion, and Bitstamp Notional Volumes\nL95@P2: which decreased 28% year-over-year to $29 billion.\nL96@P2: • Event Contracts Traded increased over 7x year-over-year to a record 16.4 billion.\nL97@P2-3: 3Conference Call and Livestream Information\nL98@P3: Robinhood will host a video call to discuss its results at 2 p.m. PT / 5 p.m. ET today, October 27, 2026. The\nL99@P3: video call can be accessed at investors.robinhood.com, along with the earnings press release and accompanying\nL100@P3: slide presentation. The event will also be live streamed to YouTube and X.com via Robinhood’s official channels,\nL101@P3: @RobinhoodApp, on Vlad Tenev’s X.com account, @vladtenev, as well as in the Robinhood App. Following the\nL102@P3: call, a replay and transcript will also be available at investors.robinhood.com.\nL103@P3: Financial Outlook\nL104@P3: The paragraph below provides information on our 2026 expense plan and outlook. We are not providing a 2026\nL105@P3: outlook for total operating expenses and have not reconciled our 2026 outlook for Adjusted Operating Expenses\nL106@P3: and SBC to the most directly comparable GAAP financial measure, total operating expenses, because we are\nL107@P3: unable to predict with reasonable certainty the impact of certain items without unreasonable effort. These items\nL108@P3: include, but are not limited to, provision for credit losses and significant regulatory expenses which may be material\nL109@P3: and could have a significant impact on total operating expenses for 2026.\nL110@P3: Our prior outlook for 2026 Adjusted Operating Expenses and SBC provided at Q2 2026 Earnings (July 29,\nL111@P3: 2026) was $2.675 to $2.775 billion. As we look ahead to the rest of the year, we are lowering the top end of our\nL112@P3: prior outlook by $25 million to a range of $2.675 to $2.75 billion to reflect ongoing efficiency gains across our\nL113@P3: business. This expense outlook does not include provision for credit losses, costs related to our pending\nL114@P3: acquisitions, costs from equity modifications of restructuring and executive awards in connection with our CFO\nL115@P3: transition, interest expenses classified as operating expenses, potential significant regulatory matters, or other\nL116@P3: significant expenses (such as impairments, restructuring charges, and other business acquisition- or disposition\u0002related expenses) that may arise or accruals we may determine in the future are required, as we are unable to\nL117@P3: accurately predict the size or timing of such matters, expenses or accruals at this time.\nL118@P3: Actual results might differ materially from our outlook due to several factors, including the rate of growth in\nL119@P3: Funded Customers and our effectiveness to cross-sell products which affects variable marketing costs, the degree\nL120@P3: to which we are successful in managing credit losses and preventing fraud, and our ability to manage web-hosting\nL121@P3: expenses efficiently, among other factors. See “Non-GAAP Financial Measures” for more information on Adjusted\nL122@P3: Operating Expenses and SBC, including significant items that we believe are not indicative of our ongoing expenses\nL123@P3: that would be adjusted out of total operating expenses (GAAP) to get to Adjusted Operating Expenses and SBC\nL124@P3: (non-GAAP) should they occur.\nL125@P3: About Robinhood\nL126@P3: Robinhood Markets, Inc. (NASDAQ: HOOD) is a global leader in financial services offering retail brokerage,\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQ3 2026 Earnings Presentation (https://investors.robinhood.com/static-files/fd90f575-5b4c-4cde-a16d-d114c920fb6a)\nciteturn50view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 49; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://investors.robinhood.com/static-files/fd90f575-5b4c-4cde-a16d-d114c920fb6a\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1610\nL0@P0: investors.robinhood.com\nL1@P0: Robinhood Markets, Inc.\nL2@P0: Earnings Presentation Third Quarter 2026\nL3@P0-1: October 27, 2026investors.robinhood.com 2\nL4@P1: Disclaimers\nL5@P1: This Presentation Relates to Robinhood’s Broader Earnings Announcement Disclosures\nL6@P1: This presentation accompanies the third quarter 2026 earnings announcement webcast of Robinhood Markets, Inc. (including its consolidated subsidiaries, “we,” “Robinhood,” or the “Company”) and should be read together with Robinhood’s earnings announcement press release. Hyperlinks to our third quarter 2026\nL7@P1: webcast and press release can be found together with these slides on Robinhood’s investor relations website at investors.robinhood.com.\nL8@P1: Key Performance Metrics\nL9@P1: This presentation includes key performance metrics that our management uses to help evaluate our business, identify trends affecting our business, formulate business plans, and make strategic decisions. Our key performance metrics include Funded Customers, Total Platform Assets, Net Deposits, Average\nL10@P1: Revenue Per User (“ARPU”), and Robinhood Gold Subscribers. Definitions of performance metrics can be found in the appendix to this presentation (the “Appendix”).\nL11@P1: Non-GAAP Financial Measures and Where to Find Reconciliations to GAAP\nL12@P1: This presentation includes financial measures that were not prepared in accordance with U.S. generally accepted accounting principles (“GAAP”). Our non-GAAP financial measures include adjusted earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation, and amortization (“Adjusted EBITDA”), Adjusted EBITDA Margin,\nL13@P1: Incremental Adjusted EBITDA Margin, Adjusted Operating Expenses, Adjusted Operating Expenses and SBC, Adjusted Net Income, Adjusted EPS, Q4 2024 Net Income Prior to Tax Benefit and Regulatory Accrual Reversal, and Q4 2024 EPS Prior to Tax Benefit and Regulatory Accrual Reversal. Definitions,\nL14@P1: explanations, and reconciliations to the most comparable GAAP financial measures can be found in the Appendix.\nL15@P1: Cautionary Note Regarding Forward-Looking Statements\nL16@P1: This presentation and the related webcast contain forward-looking statements regarding our expected financial performance and our strategic and operational plans, including (among others) statements regarding that we continue to grow and diversify our business; that we anticipate diluted share count will be roughly\nL17@P1: flat in 2026; that with corporate cash, investments, and stablecoin, we are well positioned to continue deploying capital; that we will continue to look to drive growth and shareholder value by allocating capital across organic growth, M&A, and shareholder returns; that new trading products continue to scale; that we are\nL18@P1: deepening relationships and growing our share of wallet with customers as we deliver innovative wealth management products; that the majority of the remaining Gold deposit boosts earned by customers prior to the end of the program will be paid out over a relatively short period of time; that we continue to\nL19@P1: repurchase shares over time and closely manage share-based compensation; and all statements about our FY 2026 financial outlook. Forward-looking statements generally relate to future events or our future financial or operating performance. In some cases, you can identify forward-looking statements because they\nL20@P1: contain words such as “believe,” “may,” “will,” “should,” “expect,” “plan,” “anticipate,” “could,” “intend,” “target,” “project,” “contemplate,” “estimate,” “predict,” “potential,” or “continue,” or the negative of these words or other similar terms or expressions that concern our expectations, strategy, plans, or intentions. Our\nL21@P1: forward-looking statements are subject to a number of known and unknown risks, uncertainties, assumptions, and other factors that may cause our actual future results, performance, or achievements to differ materially from any future results expressed or implied in this presentation and the related webcast. Reported\nL22@P1: results should not be considered an indication of future performance. Factors that contribute to the uncertain nature of our forward-looking statements include, among others: our rapid and continuing expansion, including continuing to introduce new products and services on our platforms as well as geographic\nL23@P1: expansion; the difficulty of managing our business effectively, including the size of our workforce, and the risk of declining or negative growth; the fluctuations in our financial results and key metrics from quarter to quarter; our reliance on transaction-based revenue, including payment for order flow (“PFOF”), the risk of\nL24@P1: new regulation or bans on PFOF and similar practices, and the addition of our new fee-based model for cryptocurrency; our exposure to fluctuations in interest rates and rapidly changing interest rate environments; the difficulty of raising additional capital (to provide liquidity needs and support business growth and\nL25@P1: objectives) on reasonable terms, if at all; the need to maintain capital levels required by regulators and self-regulatory organizations; the risk that we might mishandle the cash, securities, and cryptocurrencies we hold on behalf of customers, and our exposure to liability for processing, operational, or technical errors in\nL26@P1: clearing functions; the impact of negative publicity on our brand and reputation; the risk that changes in business, economic, or political conditions that impact the global financial markets, or a systemic market event, might harm our business; our dependence on key employees and a skilled workforce; the fact that we\nL27@P1: do not wholly own or operationally control Rothera, our joint venture with Susquehanna International Group, and its subsidiaries; operational and regulatory risks and expenditures prior to and following closing of our acquisitions and investments; the difficulty of complying with an extensive, complex, and changing\nL28@P1: regulatory environment, the risk of monetary and other penalties for noncompliance, and the need to adjust our business model in response to new or modified laws and regulations; the possibility of adverse developments in pending litigation and regulatory investigations; the risk that the outcome of currently ongoing\nL29@P1: and potential future regulatory enforcement actions and litigation, as well as potential changes in federal or state law, could immediately or subsequently prevent us from offering, or continuing to offer, event contracts or that we have in the past decided, and may in the future decide, to stop offering certain new event\nL30@P1: contracts as a result of actual or threatened state enforcement actions; the effects of competition; our need to innovate and acquire or invest in new products, services, technologies and geographies in order to attract and retain customers and deepen their engagement with us in order to maintain growth; our reliance\nL31@P1: on third parties to perform some key functions and the risk that processing, operational or technological failures could impair the availability or stability of our platforms; the risk of cybersecurity incidents, theft, data breaches, and other online attacks; the difficulty of processing customer data in compliance with privacy\nL32@P1: laws; our need as a regulated financial services company to develop and maintain effective compliance and risk management infrastructures; the risks associated with incorporating artificial intelligence technologies into some of our products and processes; the regulation, litigation, contractual, operational, and\nL33@P1: reputational risks associated with our introduction of products such as Robinhood Chain and Stock Tokens globally, continued offering of Classic Stock Tokens (formerly \"Robinhood Stock Tokens\") and perpetual futures trading in the European Economic Area, our planned offering of perpetual futures in the U.S.,\nL34@P1: updates to Robinhood Wallet, and our staking and onchain lending services offered in the U.S.; and the risk that substantial future sales of Class A common stock in the public market, or the perception that they may occur, could cause the price of our stock to fall. Because some of these risks and uncertainties cannot\nL35@P1: be predicted or quantified and some are beyond our control, you should not rely on our forward-looking statements as predictions of future events. More information about potential risks and uncertainties that could affect our business and financial results can be found in Part II, Item 1A of o